  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Weakness, numbness of the ring and little fingers, and curling of those fingers in the right hand for four weeks after a fall onto the right elbow, in a left-handed artist who uses the right hand for the palette and brushwork; stroke and a peripheral nerve lesion under evaluation</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R29.818</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting concern; it echoes, without asserting, the paralysis of his right hand recorded later in 1517 (CLM-0006). Superseded by the diagnosis at the second visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>One fall in the past year (slipped on stone stairs and landed on the right elbow), with no loss of consciousness, dizziness, or palpitations before it; fall risk</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z91.81</td><td>S — Synthetic fall that anchors the modern ulnar nerve injury; no historical fall is asserted.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Ulnar neuropathy at the right elbow (cubital tunnel), post-traumatic, moderately severe: sensory loss in the ulnar fingers and dorsal ulnar hand, weakness and early wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles with clawing of the ring and little fingers, focal slowing and partial conduction block across the elbow, and active denervation on EMG; stroke, C8 radiculopathy, and lower trunk plexopathy excluded</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G56.21</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis. The paralysis of his right hand in 1517 is documented (D); its cause is unknown, and ulnar palsy, stroke, and median nerve palsy are competing retrospective readings (R).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Primary hypercholesterolemia (LDL 146 mg/dL) with a Pooled Cohort Equations 10-year ASCVD risk of 13.5 percent (PREVENT 6.9 percent); found on the stroke risk screen</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E78.00</td><td>S — Synthetic risk factor found on the stroke risk screen; no historical lipid disorder is asserted.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

