    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Ulnar neuropathy at the right elbow (cubital tunnel), post-traumatic (G56.21)</content>. Early sensory improvement (two-point discrimination 10 to 8 mm), no motor change yet, which is expected four weeks into conservative treatment. QuickDASH 49, from 52. Hand therapy started two weeks late. [S]<list><item>Continue both splints and hand therapy twice a week.</item><item>Surgical review at the next visit, about three months from the injury.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Primary hypercholesterolemia (E78.00)</content>. Four weeks of atorvastatin 20 mg without muscle symptoms. [S]<list><item>Continue atorvastatin; lipid panel at the next visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ulnar motor and sensory examination: strength of the first dorsal interosseous, abductor digiti minimi, and deep flexor of the little finger (MRC grade), two-point discrimination of the little finger, Froment and Wartenberg signs, clawing, and wasting</td><td>Every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two-point discrimination 8 mm at the little finger; first dorsal interosseous 3/5, abductor digiti minimi 3/5, deep flexor 4/5; mild clawing</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Grip and key-pinch dynamometry, both hands</td><td>Every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Grip 21 kg right, 38 kg left; key pinch 3.2 kg right</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Patient-reported hand function (QuickDASH)</td><td>Before treatment, then at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>49 of 100, from 52</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Splint use and avoiding pressure on and prolonged bending of the elbow</td><td>Every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Both splints worn as advised; elbow kept off the table</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Hand therapy</td><td>Twice a week from diagnosis</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Late start: the first session was two weeks after the referral rather than within the week, because the hand therapist had no openings. Outcome: splints had been fitted at diagnosis, and the delay has not changed the course so far; four sessions done. Corrective action: hand-therapy requests for nerve injuries now go on the urgent list, and he has a fixed twice-weekly slot</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Repeat nerve conduction studies</td><td>Only if the clinical course is unclear or before surgery when the baseline is old</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>The diagnosis is clear and the studies are six weeks old</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Corticosteroid injection at the cubital tunnel</td><td>Not part of treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No benefit over placebo at three months in the Cochrane review</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Statin adherence and muscle symptoms</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses; no muscle aches</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel after starting a statin</td><td>4 to 12 weeks after starting or changing</td><td>Due</td><td>Four weeks after the start; planned at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, weight, and smoking status</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>126/76 mmHg; 73.8 kg; never smoker</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stroke excluded and the lesion localized by the second visit. Outcome by 2026-08-17: Normal neurological examination apart from the right ulnar territory; MRI brain normal; nerve conduction studies, EMG, and ultrasound confirm ulnar neuropathy at the elbow [S]</item><item>Keep working: drawing, writing, and teaching with the left hand, and painting with adapted tools and help. In progress; target 2027-02-01 [S]</item><item>Recovery of ulnar sensation and intrinsic strength, judged at about three months from the injury. In progress; target 2027-02-01 [S]</item><item>LDL down by 30 to 49 percent on a moderate-intensity statin. In progress; target 2026-10-20 [S]</item><item>No further falls. In progress; target 2027-08-01 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Cubital Tunnel Syndrome (Ulnar Nerve Entrapment at the Elbow), OrthoInfo (2026); Treatment for ulnar neuropathy at the elbow (Caliandro P, et al.) (2025); Practice parameter for electrodiagnostic studies in ulnar neuropathy at the elbow: summary statement (American Association of Electrodiagnostic Medicine, American Academy of Neurology, American Academy of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation) (1999); 2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol (2018); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hyperlipidemia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Surgical review and lipid panel in about five weeks; wellness visit next month. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-01: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
